1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,108
The Southern Special Students:

2
00:00:03,188 --> 00:00:07,650
Malayan Youths Sent to Japan, 1943 to 1945

3
00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:11,952
On the 4th of June 1943 the
front page of the Syonan Shimbun

4
00:00:12,032 --> 00:00:16,334
reported that 33 "potential
leaders" from Malaya and Sumatra

5
00:00:16,414 --> 00:00:20,644
were being trained at Woodsville,
off Upper Serangoon Road,

6
00:00:20,724 --> 00:00:25,325
in Japanese language, physical
culture and "the Nippon spirit".

7
00:00:25,325 --> 00:00:28,283
The fifteen with the best results would

8
00:00:28,362 --> 00:00:31,400
go on to three years of study in Japan.

9
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:34,541
Two of the 33 were from Syonan itself,

10
00:00:34,621 --> 00:00:38,350
"two Malai youths named
Bostam and Kasmani".

11
00:00:38,350 --> 00:00:42,363
Over the next two years
about 200 young people from

12
00:00:42,443 --> 00:00:48,383
across occupied Southeast Asia went to
Japan as Nanpō tokubetsu ryūgakusei,

13
00:00:48,463 --> 00:00:50,550
Southern Special Students.

14
00:00:50,550 --> 00:00:54,453
A few of them ended up at
a university in Hiroshima,

15
00:00:54,533 --> 00:00:58,207
and two of the Malayans
among them died after the

16
00:00:58,287 --> 00:01:02,500
atomic bomb fell on the city
on the 6th of August 1945.

17
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:05,711
The plan was announced in the Syonan

18
00:01:05,791 --> 00:01:08,900
Shimbun on the 13th of April 1943.

19
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:13,277
The Gunseikanbu, the military
administration, would send fifteen

20
00:01:13,357 --> 00:01:16,898
promising students from
Malaya and Sumatra to Japan,

21
00:01:16,978 --> 00:01:21,563
"in order to let them get in touch
with the essence of the national

22
00:01:21,643 --> 00:01:26,100
characteristics as well as the
national constitution of Nippon".

23
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:29,587
On their return they were
to work with the Japanese

24
00:01:29,667 --> 00:01:33,153
authorities "at the head
of the local inhabitants",

25
00:01:33,233 --> 00:01:36,800
towards the Greater East
Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere.

26
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:41,344
Only young men aged 17 to 20
could apply, and they had to be

27
00:01:41,424 --> 00:01:46,125
"qualified to be the leaders of
their communities in future".

28
00:01:46,125 --> 00:01:48,600
The terms were generous.

29
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:51,476
The administration paid for the journey,

30
00:01:51,556 --> 00:01:56,798
and the Greater East Asia Ministry
in Tokyo, working with the education,

31
00:01:56,878 --> 00:01:59,976
army and navy ministries,
gave each student

32
00:02:00,056 --> 00:02:03,825
a monthly allowance of 100
yen for school expenses.

33
00:02:03,825 --> 00:02:07,340
The students would spend
a year learning Japanese

34
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:10,495
and then study a subject
such as education,

35
00:02:10,575 --> 00:02:13,950
industrial technique or
police administration.

36
00:02:13,950 --> 00:02:18,557
Each state's Chief Administrator
would pick one or two candidates

37
00:02:18,637 --> 00:02:22,675
and recommend them to the
Gunseikan by the end of April.

38
00:02:22,675 --> 00:02:26,825
The aims were stated
just as plainly in Tokyo.

39
00:02:26,825 --> 00:02:30,984
In February 1944 Kazuo Aoki, the
Minister for Greater East Asia,

40
00:02:31,064 --> 00:02:35,156
told a budget committee of the
House of Peers that the students

41
00:02:35,236 --> 00:02:39,263
would be guided towards "complete
faith in Nippon and complete

42
00:02:39,343 --> 00:02:44,097
understanding of its policies", so
that they would "co-operate more fully

43
00:02:44,177 --> 00:02:48,350
with Nippon" when they took up
positions of leadership at home.

44
00:02:48,350 --> 00:02:51,576
Japan was training an elite for the order

45
00:02:51,656 --> 00:02:54,800
it intended to build in Southeast Asia.

46
00:02:54,800 --> 00:03:00,510
The first course began on the 10th of
May 1943 at the Ryugakusei Kunrenjo,

47
00:03:00,590 --> 00:03:05,050
the training school for overseas
students, at Woodsville.

48
00:03:05,050 --> 00:03:08,206
Fourteen of the trainees came from Malaya,

49
00:03:08,286 --> 00:03:11,600
seventeen from Sumatra
and two from Syonan.

50
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:16,320
The newspaper said that all 33
had been "carefully selected from

51
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:21,725
respectable families" and had received
"a sound preliminary education".

52
00:03:21,725 --> 00:03:25,464
Their instructors, Kyoji
Sunagawa and Mitsuo Matsunaga,

53
00:03:25,544 --> 00:03:30,046
taught mainly Japanese and physical
training, so that the students

54
00:03:30,126 --> 00:03:33,875
could "assimilate the Nippon
spirit" before they left.

55
00:03:33,875 --> 00:03:38,150
The fifteen who were chosen
left before the course ended.

56
00:03:38,150 --> 00:03:42,765
On the 30th of June 1943 a
party of students from Burma,

57
00:03:42,845 --> 00:03:47,543
Malaya, Sumatra and Java
arrived at Tokyo Station dressed

58
00:03:47,623 --> 00:03:52,150
in Japan's national civilian
uniform, the kokuminfuku.

59
00:03:52,150 --> 00:03:56,279
They went straight to the plaza
in front of the Imperial Palace

60
00:03:56,359 --> 00:04:00,153
to pay their respects to the
Emperor, and then enrolled at

61
00:04:00,233 --> 00:04:04,175
the Kokusai Gakuyukai, the
International Students' Society.

62
00:04:04,175 --> 00:04:09,660
The 18 who stayed behind received their
certificates at Woodsville in July,

63
00:04:09,740 --> 00:04:13,147
at a ceremony that began
with a bow towards the

64
00:04:13,227 --> 00:04:17,975
Imperial Palace and the singing
of the Japanese national anthem.

65
00:04:17,975 --> 00:04:22,029
The newspaper does not say
whether Bostam and Kasmani were

66
00:04:22,109 --> 00:04:26,600
among the fifteen, and neither
name appears in its pages again.

67
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:30,075
Some Malayans were in Japan already.

68
00:04:30,075 --> 00:04:35,328
On the 1st of June 1943 the paper
reported that two Johor students,

69
00:04:35,408 --> 00:04:39,467
Ungku Aziz and Ungku Mohsen,
had passed the entrance

70
00:04:39,547 --> 00:04:44,403
examinations for Waseda University
in Tokyo, and that a third,

71
00:04:44,483 --> 00:04:49,975
Wan Abdul Hamid, was applying to
the Tokyo University of Agriculture.

72
00:04:49,975 --> 00:04:53,853
Two young women from Malaya
were studying there as well,

73
00:04:53,933 --> 00:04:57,750
and all five lived at the
mansion of Marquis Tokugawa.

74
00:04:57,750 --> 00:05:01,468
By August 1943 the paper
counted 20 students

75
00:05:01,548 --> 00:05:05,000
from Malaya in Japan, two of them women.

76
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:08,459
The second year's selection
was smaller and was run

77
00:05:08,539 --> 00:05:12,425
through the Koa Kunrensho, a
training school in Malacca.

78
00:05:12,425 --> 00:05:16,154
In January 1944 the
administration announced that ten

79
00:05:16,234 --> 00:05:19,101
students would be chosen on state quotas,

80
00:05:19,181 --> 00:05:23,917
with preference for those with a
good education, everyday Japanese,

81
00:05:23,997 --> 00:05:28,525
and "whose absence will be no
inconvenience to their families".

82
00:05:28,525 --> 00:05:32,618
In April the paper named the
four who had been picked "from

83
00:05:32,698 --> 00:05:36,509
all parts of Malai": Ibrahim
bin Mahat, 18, from Johor,

84
00:05:36,589 --> 00:05:40,399
to study chemistry; Hassan
bin Ahmat, 17, from Malacca,

85
00:05:40,479 --> 00:05:46,058
for agriculture; Yusoffe bin Abdul Kadir,
20, from Penang, also for agriculture;

86
00:05:46,138 --> 00:05:49,675
and Abdul Razak, 18, from
Selangor, for education.

87
00:05:49,675 --> 00:05:54,532
A second group of 89 Southern
Special Students arrived in

88
00:05:54,612 --> 00:05:59,550
Tokyo on the 10th of June
1944, four of them from Malaya.

89
00:05:59,550 --> 00:06:04,775
That Abdul Razak was almost
certainly Abdul Razak Abdul Hamid,

90
00:06:04,855 --> 00:06:10,679
who was born in Penang in 1925 and
who, Hiroshima University records,

91
00:06:10,759 --> 00:06:15,550
came to Japan in 1944 as a
government-sponsored student.

92
00:06:15,550 --> 00:06:19,731
Nik Yusof Nik Ali, from Kota
Bharu, and Syed Omar Syed

93
00:06:19,811 --> 00:06:24,625
Mohammad Alsagoff were also
among the Malayans in the scheme.

94
00:06:24,625 --> 00:06:28,524
Altogether about 205 young
people came to Japan from

95
00:06:28,604 --> 00:06:32,886
Southeast Asia as Southern
Special Students, from Malaya,

96
00:06:32,966 --> 00:06:37,175
Indonesia, Burma, the
Philippines, Thailand and Brunei.

97
00:06:37,175 --> 00:06:41,760
The students spent their first
year at the International Students

98
00:06:41,840 --> 00:06:45,993
Institute in Tokyo, learning
Japanese and Japanese history,

99
00:06:46,073 --> 00:06:51,025
and were then placed at universities
and colleges across the country.

100
00:06:51,025 --> 00:06:55,037
They lived in dormitories
run for them by the society,

101
00:06:55,117 --> 00:06:59,205
and from mid-1944 a housing
society placed some of them

102
00:06:59,285 --> 00:07:03,150
with Japanese families as
members of the household.

103
00:07:03,150 --> 00:07:06,275
But the war was turning against Japan.

104
00:07:06,275 --> 00:07:09,410
Food was short, and from November

105
00:07:09,490 --> 00:07:12,900
1944 American bombers raided Tokyo.

106
00:07:12,900 --> 00:07:17,107
By the spring of 1945 nine
Southern Special Students were

107
00:07:17,187 --> 00:07:21,845
enrolled at the Hiroshima University
of Literature and Science,

108
00:07:21,925 --> 00:07:25,906
a teacher-training university,
among them Abdul Razak,

109
00:07:25,986 --> 00:07:31,025
Nik Yusof and Syed Omar from Malaya
and Pengiran Yusuf from Brunei.

110
00:07:31,025 --> 00:07:35,650
Abdul Razak enrolled in April 1945.

111
00:07:35,650 --> 00:07:39,428
The overseas students lived
at the Konan dormitory,

112
00:07:39,508 --> 00:07:44,500
about 900 metres from the point
over which the bomb would explode.

113
00:07:44,500 --> 00:07:49,167
At 8.15 on the morning of
the 6th of August 1945 an

114
00:07:49,247 --> 00:07:53,900
American B-29 dropped an
atomic bomb on Hiroshima.

115
00:07:53,900 --> 00:07:56,866
Eight of the nine
Southern Special Students

116
00:07:56,946 --> 00:07:59,425
at the university were in the city.

117
00:07:59,425 --> 00:08:02,955
Abdul Razak was in a lecture room about

118
00:08:03,035 --> 00:08:06,275
1.5 kilometres from the hypocentre.

119
00:08:06,275 --> 00:08:08,797
The building collapsed on top of him,

120
00:08:08,877 --> 00:08:12,675
and he lost consciousness
and woke to a city in ruins.

121
00:08:12,675 --> 00:08:17,500
Nik Yusof, who was 19, was
at the Konan dormitory.

122
00:08:17,500 --> 00:08:22,524
He got out of the collapsed building
and set off west towards Itsukaichi,

123
00:08:22,604 --> 00:08:25,750
on the edge of the city,
but died on the way.

124
00:08:25,750 --> 00:08:30,080
An urn bearing his name was
left in the care of Kozenji,

125
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:33,625
a Buddhist temple in
what is now Saeki Ward.

126
00:08:33,625 --> 00:08:36,193
The surviving students slept in the

127
00:08:36,273 --> 00:08:39,300
open on the university grounds for days.

128
00:08:39,300 --> 00:08:43,617
Meiko Kurihara, a 19-year-old who
had fled there after finding her

129
00:08:43,697 --> 00:08:47,615
home burned down, later remembered
that they encouraged her,

130
00:08:47,695 --> 00:08:52,425
helped her search for her missing
father and shared what food they had.

131
00:08:52,425 --> 00:08:56,029
Syed Omar, also 19, developed
a fever while helping

132
00:08:56,109 --> 00:08:59,641
to care for the wounded,
and her mother cooled his

133
00:08:59,721 --> 00:09:03,550
head with a wet handkerchief
as he lay on the ground.

134
00:09:03,550 --> 00:09:06,897
After Japan's surrender
on the 15th of August

135
00:09:06,977 --> 00:09:10,175
the students began to make their way home.

136
00:09:10,175 --> 00:09:13,123
Syed Omar fell ill on the journey and

137
00:09:13,203 --> 00:09:16,150
was admitted to a hospital in Kyoto.

138
00:09:16,150 --> 00:09:20,800
A doctor there, Hamajima
Yoshihiro, gave him his own blood.

139
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:25,167
Syed Omar is reported to have
told him, "You and I are brothers

140
00:09:25,247 --> 00:09:29,200
because you gave me your blood
to try and save my life."

141
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:32,131
He died at the beginning of September;

142
00:09:32,211 --> 00:09:37,600
the hospital's death certificate
is dated the 3rd of September 1945.

143
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:41,416
Abdul Razak wrote to Kurihara
that his friend's death was

144
00:09:41,496 --> 00:09:45,106
"undoubtedly due to the
effects from the atomic bomb",

145
00:09:45,186 --> 00:09:50,175
and added that he now had a cough
himself and might follow the same fate.

146
00:09:50,175 --> 00:09:52,789
The Southern Special Students went home

147
00:09:52,869 --> 00:09:55,425
to countries that were changing fast.

148
00:09:55,425 --> 00:10:00,306
The three young men from Johor who
had gone before the scheme began

149
00:10:00,386 --> 00:10:05,562
were already back: in April 1945 the
Syonan Shimbun reported that Aziz,

150
00:10:05,642 --> 00:10:09,708
Ungku Mohsin and Wan Abdul
Hamid had returned after more

151
00:10:09,788 --> 00:10:14,675
than two years in Japan and joined
the Johor government's service.

152
00:10:14,675 --> 00:10:19,675
The paper quoted Aziz praising
the Japanese family system.

153
00:10:19,675 --> 00:10:23,476
He was Ungku Abdul Aziz,
who went on to study

154
00:10:23,556 --> 00:10:27,184
economics at Raffles
College after the war,

155
00:10:27,264 --> 00:10:32,703
earned his doctorate from Waseda
in 1964 and was Vice-Chancellor

156
00:10:32,783 --> 00:10:36,750
of the University of
Malaya from 1968 to 1988.

157
00:10:36,750 --> 00:10:41,561
Pengiran Muhammad Yusuf, who
had been selected in late 1943

158
00:10:41,641 --> 00:10:46,535
with other students from Borneo,
suffered radiation sickness

159
00:10:46,615 --> 00:10:51,175
after the bombing and returned
to Brunei in March 1946.

160
00:10:51,175 --> 00:10:55,404
Writing as Yura Halim, he
wrote the words of "Allah

161
00:10:55,484 --> 00:10:59,376
Peliharakan Sultan" in
1947, which later became

162
00:10:59,456 --> 00:11:03,601
Brunei's national anthem,
and from 1968 to 1973 he

163
00:11:03,681 --> 00:11:08,075
was the country's menteri
besar, its chief minister.

164
00:11:08,075 --> 00:11:12,370
Abdul Razak Abdul Hamid
became a teacher and spent his

165
00:11:12,450 --> 00:11:16,825
working life building ties
between Malaysia and Japan.

166
00:11:16,825 --> 00:11:20,677
He became a professor of
Japanese studies, set up the

167
00:11:20,757 --> 00:11:25,796
Japanese-language programme at what
is now Universiti Teknologi MARA,

168
00:11:25,876 --> 00:11:30,321
and in 1982 was put in charge
of Malaysia's Look East Policy,

169
00:11:30,401 --> 00:11:35,075
which sent Malaysian students
and trainees to learn from Japan.

170
00:11:35,075 --> 00:11:39,125
He wrote about Hiroshima in
a memoir, Debu Hiroshima (The

171
00:11:39,205 --> 00:11:43,625
Ashes of Hiroshima), and spoke
often against nuclear weapons.

172
00:11:43,625 --> 00:11:47,631
Hiroshima University
awarded him an honorary

173
00:11:47,711 --> 00:11:51,531
doctorate in February 2013, and he died in

174
00:11:51,611 --> 00:11:55,975
Kuala Lumpur on the 18th
of July 2013, aged 88.

175
00:11:55,975 --> 00:11:58,828
The scheme set out to
train young people who

176
00:11:58,908 --> 00:12:02,362
would lead their countries
in partnership with Japan,

177
00:12:02,442 --> 00:12:04,975
and several of them did go on to lead.

178
00:12:04,975 --> 00:12:07,479
But they led independent countries,

179
00:12:07,559 --> 00:12:11,250
in a world that Japan's
planners had not foreseen.

180
00:12:11,250 --> 00:12:14,092
The two who died were remembered first by

181
00:12:14,172 --> 00:12:17,450
Japanese people who had
no connection to them.

182
00:12:17,450 --> 00:12:21,339
In 1961 Kenkichi Sonobe,
a resident of Kyoto,

183
00:12:21,419 --> 00:12:27,249
built an Islamic-style grave for
Syed Omar at a temple in the city,

184
00:12:27,329 --> 00:12:31,475
and in 1974 he formed a
group to look after it.

185
00:12:31,475 --> 00:12:35,087
His daughter, Toshie
Sonobe, has gone on visiting

186
00:12:35,167 --> 00:12:38,704
the grave since the group
was dissolved in 2008,

187
00:12:38,784 --> 00:12:42,325
and a memorial service
is held there each year.

188
00:12:42,325 --> 00:12:47,237
In Hiroshima the head priest of
Kozenji raised an Islamic-style

189
00:12:47,317 --> 00:12:51,595
grave over Nik Yusof's remains
in 1964, the only Muslim

190
00:12:51,675 --> 00:12:56,350
grave at the temple, and a
service is held there regularly.

191
00:12:56,350 --> 00:13:00,722
In 2019 a group from Kota
Bharu, Nik Yusof's home town,

192
00:13:00,802 --> 00:13:06,225
among them a relative of his,
visited the grave for the first time.

193
00:13:06,225 --> 00:13:10,815
Hiroshima University has taken
part in the memorial service

194
00:13:10,895 --> 00:13:15,405
for Syed Omar since the 1970s,
and in 2015 it gave special

195
00:13:15,485 --> 00:13:18,888
awards to Toshie Sonobe
and to Hiroso Niimi,

196
00:13:18,968 --> 00:13:24,350
a former member of its staff, for
keeping the students' story alive.

197
00:13:24,350 --> 00:13:29,120
In 2023 the university applied to
Hiroshima City to have the names

198
00:13:29,200 --> 00:13:33,529
of overseas students who died
after the bombing, from China,

199
00:13:33,609 --> 00:13:39,481
Mongolia and Southeast Asia, added to the
city's register of atomic bomb victims,

200
00:13:39,561 --> 00:13:43,750
which is kept in the cenotaph
at the Peace Memorial Park.

201
00:13:43,750 --> 00:13:48,575
Normally only a victim's family
can make such an application.

202
00:13:48,575 --> 00:13:53,150
The city accepted the request,
the first time that had been done.

203
00:13:53,150 --> 00:13:56,982
Japan still pays for students
from Southeast Asia to

204
00:13:57,062 --> 00:14:01,200
study in its universities,
but on very different terms.

205
00:14:01,200 --> 00:14:05,745
The Japanese Government
(Monbukagakusho) Scholarship began in

206
00:14:05,825 --> 00:14:10,370
1954 and has since brought tens
of thousands of students from

207
00:14:10,450 --> 00:14:15,075
around the world, Singaporeans
among them, to study in Japan.

208
00:14:15,075 --> 00:14:19,475
Students apply for it themselves
and choose what they study.

209
00:14:19,475 --> 00:14:23,903
At the start of 1986, straight
after graduating from NUS,

210
00:14:23,983 --> 00:14:28,410
I went to Japan for 18 months
on a scholarship from AOTS,

211
00:14:28,490 --> 00:14:34,183
an organisation set up in 1959 with
the support of Japan's trade ministry

212
00:14:34,263 --> 00:14:38,850
to train engineers and managers
from developing countries.

213
00:14:38,850 --> 00:14:42,595
I began with Nihongo lessons
at the Yokohama Kenshu

214
00:14:42,675 --> 00:14:46,345
Center and then trained
at TOPACS, a Tokyo company

215
00:14:46,425 --> 00:14:50,175
that wrote real-time
software for chemical plants.

216
00:14:50,175 --> 00:14:54,273
It was more than forty years
after Bostam and Kasmani were

217
00:14:54,353 --> 00:14:58,595
chosen at Woodsville, and by
then Japan came to Singapore as

218
00:14:58,675 --> 00:15:02,925
an investor and trading partner
rather than as an occupier.

219
00:15:02,925 --> 00:15:07,220
Where it fits in the bigger story:
the Southern Special Students

220
00:15:07,300 --> 00:15:10,296
are a small part of the
Occupation's history,

221
00:15:10,376 --> 00:15:15,834
but the newspapers of 1943 and 1944 show
clearly what the scheme was meant to do:

222
00:15:15,914 --> 00:15:19,662
to take promising young people
from Singapore and Malaya

223
00:15:19,742 --> 00:15:23,775
and shape them into leaders
loyal to Japan's order in Asia.

224
00:15:23,775 --> 00:15:27,101
The war ended before that
order could take hold,

225
00:15:27,181 --> 00:15:31,225
and two of the young men died
